From fa9d3e545ab64637ae3aea02bd735918be2f538c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: LR Recomp <noreply@lr-recomp.invalid>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 01:04:51 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 043/108] gpu: vulkan: optionally restrict render target cache
 render areas (vulkan_render_area_restrict)

Every render target cache render pass instance used the whole framebuffer
as its render area, and the framebuffer is as tall as the whole EDRAM for
its pitch (1280x2048 for a 1280-wide target the game draws 720 rows of),
so with LOAD / STORE attachments a tile-based GPU loaded and stored ~2.8x
the needed pixels in each of ~84 passes per frame.

With vulkan_render_area_restrict (default off), the union of everything a
command in the instance may write is tracked: the dynamic scissor of each
guest draw (the whole framebuffer if the scissor state is unknown), the
geometry rectangles of render target ownership transfer draws (their
scissor is the whole framebuffer), and the vkCmdClearAttachments rectangles
of stencil clears and resolve clears. When the instance ends, the union,
aligned to 80x16 pixels (whole EDRAM tiles for every sample count and bpp)
and clamped to the framebuffer, is written into the begin command already
recorded in the deferred command buffer (VkRenderPassBeginInfo or
VkRenderingInfo). The instance is never split or re-begun, so the pass
structure is identical to the cvar being off; pixels outside the area are
neither loaded nor stored and keep their contents, as before.

The 10-s vulkan_gpu_timing line gains the average render area, framebuffer
size, pixel coverage (actual and what the writes need) and the number of
restricted passes per frame.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

